# [Scenario] Build a notification bell that updates live, shows a popup for urgent alerts, and keeps the unread count correct even if the user has several tabs open

## 0. Before anything else: three basic ideas this scenario depends on

Since these come up constantly here, let's get them straight first, in plain terms, before touching the actual problem.

**What's a WebSocket, and why not just ask the server repeatedly (polling)?**

Normally, a browser talks to a server like this: the browser asks a question ("any new notifications?"), the server answers, and the connection closes. If you want "live" updates the old-fashioned way, you'd just ask that question over and over — every 5 seconds, say. That's called **polling**. It works, but it's wasteful: most of the time the answer is "no, nothing new," so you're making a lot of pointless round trips.

A **WebSocket** is a different kind of connection: the browser and server open one connection and *keep it open*. Instead of the browser having to ask "anything new?" repeatedly, the server can just push data down that same open connection the instant something happens. No repeated asking, no wasted requests. Think of polling as calling someone every 5 seconds to ask "any news?", versus a WebSocket as just staying on the phone with them so they can tell you the moment there's news.

**What's a browser "tab" in this context, and why does it matter?**

If a user opens your app in two browser tabs, those are two completely separate running copies of your JavaScript app. They don't automatically know anything about each other — no shared variables, nothing. If each tab opens its own WebSocket connection (because each tab runs its own copy of your code), that's genuinely two separate connections to your server, for what is really just one human being. Multiply that by however many tabs people tend to leave open, and your server ends up holding way more open connections than you actually have users.

**What's BroadcastChannel?**

It's a simple built-in browser feature that lets tabs from the *same website* send messages directly to each other, instantly, without going through your server at all. Tab A can say "hey, something changed," and every other open tab of that same site hears it immediately. That's the tool this scenario uses to keep multiple tabs in agreement with each other.

With those three ideas in hand, the actual scenario will make a lot more sense.

## 1. What is it?

The ask from the product team has three parts:

1. A bell icon that updates the instant a new notification arrives — no manual refreshing.
2. If a notification is marked "urgent," show a popup (a "toast") right away.
3. If the user has the app open in several tabs at once, all of those tabs should agree on the unread count at all times — no tab should be showing stale or wrong numbers.

The design that satisfies all three has three pieces working together:

1. **One live connection** (a WebSocket) that the server uses to push new notifications the moment they happen, instead of the browser repeatedly polling for them.
2. **Only one tab actually holds that connection** — even if the user has 5 tabs open, only 1 of them talks to the server directly. This trick is called **leader election**, and the browser gives us a tool for it called the **Web Locks API**.
3. **The one tab that's connected shares everything it learns with all the other tabs**, using **BroadcastChannel**, so every tab's bell count and notification list stay identical no matter which tab actually heard from the server first.

## 2. Why is this the right design? (What goes wrong with the simpler version?)

The simplest possible version of this feature: every tab just opens its own WebSocket connection straight to the server, and keeps its own local list of notifications. Let's walk through why that quietly falls apart.

**Problem 1: wasted connections.** If a user has 5 tabs of your app open, that's 5 separate live connections to your server for one person. Do that across thousands of users and you're paying (in server resources) for far more connections than you have actual humans using the app.

**Problem 2: tabs disagree with each other.** Say the user marks a notification as read in Tab A. Tab A now knows it's read. But Tab B has its own separate copy of the notification list sitting in its own memory — it has no way of knowing anything happened in Tab A. So Tab B's bell keeps showing the old, now-wrong unread count. Nothing will ever fix that unless you specifically build something to pass that information between tabs.

**Problem 3: urgent alerts get inconsistent.** If each tab is independently deciding whether to pop up a toast, you can end up with confusing situations — a toast showing in one tab but not another, or logic that only shows it in "whichever tab happens to be active," which isn't reliable.

So: the naive version has too many connections, and no way for tabs to agree with each other. The fix needs to solve both at once — that's exactly what leader election + BroadcastChannel gives you: **one connection total** (solves problem 1), and **a way for every tab to hear about every change instantly** (solves problems 2 and 3).

## 3. How does it actually work?

Let's build this up one small piece at a time.

**Piece 1: picking exactly one tab to be "in charge" (leader election)**

We need a way for a group of tabs, who don't know about each other yet, to agree on exactly one of them being "the leader" — the one that actually opens the WebSocket. The browser has a built-in tool for exactly this kind of "only one of you gets to do this" situation: the **Web Locks API**. Think of it like a bathroom key at a small shop — there's only one key, whoever has it is "in there," and everyone else just waits their turn automatically.

```ts
navigator.locks.request('notifications-leader-lock', () => {
  // Only ONE tab's code ever gets to run this function at a time.
  // Every other tab that asks for this same lock just waits.
  becomeLeader();
  return new Promise(() => {}); // this never finishes, so we never give the lock back
});
```

Every tab, when it starts up, asks for this same named lock (`'notifications-leader-lock'`). The browser guarantees only one tab can hold it at once — you don't have to write any code to prevent two tabs from both thinking they're the leader; the browser handles that for you. Whichever tab gets the lock becomes the **leader** and opens the real WebSocket connection. Every other tab is a **follower** and doesn't open any connection at all — it just waits.

Here's the elegant part: if the leader tab is closed, the browser automatically lets go of that lock (nobody has to write cleanup code for this), and whichever follower was waiting immediately gets it and becomes the new leader. Handoff, for free.

**Piece 2: telling every other tab what the leader just learned (BroadcastChannel)**

Only the leader hears from the server. So the leader needs to pass that information along to every other tab. That's what BroadcastChannel is for:

```ts
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('notifications-channel');

// When the leader gets a new notification from the server, it shares it:
channel.postMessage({ type: 'NEW_NOTIFICATION', notification });

// Every tab (this code runs the same way in every tab) listens for those shares:
channel.onmessage = (event) => applyMessage(event.data);
```

Every tab — leader and followers alike — runs the exact same "when I hear about a change, here's what I do" function (`applyMessage`). That's a nice side effect of this design: you only have to write the "update my bell count" logic once, and it works the same way regardless of whether this particular tab happens to be the leader or not.

This channel is used in both directions. Not just "new notification arrived," but also "user marked something as read" — whichever tab the user actually clicked "mark as read" in broadcasts that fact, and every other tab updates its own count to match. *This* is actually the piece that keeps the count "correct across tabs" — not just the incoming notifications, but every kind of change.

**Piece 3: urgent notifications pop a toast, everywhere**

Every notification has a `priority` field. The shared update function checks it:

```ts
if (notification.priority === 'urgent') {
  toastService.show(notification.message);
}
```

Since every tab runs this same check off the same shared message, an urgent notification pops up in *every* open tab — which is exactly the behavior you'd want. If something urgent happened, every window the user might be looking at should say so.

**Piece 4: don't start from a blank slate**

BroadcastChannel only tells a tab about things that happen *while that tab is open and listening*. If a tab opens after some notifications already exist — or the user was offline and reopens the app — BroadcastChannel alone won't tell it about anything from before. So on startup, every tab also makes one normal API call (`GET /notifications`) to fetch "here's everything as of right now" from the server, and only relies on the live connection/BroadcastChannel for updates from that point onward.

## 4. Example

**Live demo:** https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-nrj9sr63

The demo can't use a real server, so it fakes the "server pushing new notifications" part with a simple repeating timer (`setInterval`) instead of a real WebSocket. Everything else — the leader election, the BroadcastChannel sharing, the toast popping, the unread count — is the real, actual mechanism, not faked. Open the link in two separate browser tabs side by side: one will label itself "LEADER," the other "FOLLOWER," and you'll see the bell count update identically in both as fake notifications arrive.

```ts
// notification.service.ts
import { Injectable, NgZone, computed, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { ToastService } from './toast.service';

export interface AppNotification {
  id: string;
  message: string;
  priority: 'normal' | 'urgent';
  createdAt: number;
  read: boolean;
}

// These are the only three kinds of messages tabs ever send each other.
type BroadcastMessage =
  | { type: 'NEW_NOTIFICATION'; notification: AppNotification }
  | { type: 'MARK_READ'; id: string }
  | { type: 'MARK_ALL_READ' };

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class NotificationService {
  private notificationsState = signal<AppNotification[]>([]);
  notifications = this.notificationsState.asReadonly();
  unreadCount = computed(() => this.notificationsState().filter(n => !n.read).length);
  isLeader = signal(false);

  private channel = new BroadcastChannel('notifications-channel');
  private mockServerTimer: any = null;
  private mockIdCounter = 0;

  constructor(private toast: ToastService, private zone: NgZone) {
    // This tab listens for messages other tabs send. (A tab never receives
    // its own messages back - more on that in the traps section below.)
    this.channel.onmessage = (event: MessageEvent<BroadcastMessage>) => {
      this.zone.run(() => this.applyMessage(event.data));
    };

    // Stands in for the real "GET /notifications" call every tab makes on load,
    // so a freshly opened tab isn't starting from nothing.
    this.notificationsState.set([
      { id: 'seed-1', message: 'Your invoice #4021 was approved', priority: 'normal', createdAt: Date.now() - 60000, read: false }
    ]);

    this.electLeader();
  }

  private electLeader(): void {
    if (!('locks' in navigator)) {
      // Very old browsers without Web Locks: just fall back to "always leader."
      this.zone.run(() => this.becomeLeader());
      return;
    }
    (navigator as any).locks.request('notifications-leader-lock', () => {
      // IMPORTANT Angular-specific gotcha: this callback runs OUTSIDE
      // Angular's change-detection zone, because zone.js doesn't know about
      // navigator.locks. If we write to a signal here without NgZone.run(),
      // the data updates correctly but the screen just won't refresh.
      this.zone.run(() => this.becomeLeader());
      return new Promise(() => {}); // never resolving = never releasing the lock
    });
  }

  private becomeLeader(): void {
    this.isLeader.set(true);
    // In a real app, this is where you'd open the actual WebSocket connection.
    // Here we fake the server "pushing" a new notification every 8 seconds.
    this.mockServerTimer = setInterval(() => {
      this.mockIdCounter++;
      const isUrgent = this.mockIdCounter % 3 === 0;
      const notification: AppNotification = {
        id: 'live-' + Date.now() + '-' + this.mockIdCounter,
        message: isUrgent
          ? 'Urgent: Payment failed on order #' + (8000 + this.mockIdCounter)
          : 'New comment on your ticket #' + (1000 + this.mockIdCounter),
        priority: isUrgent ? 'urgent' : 'normal',
        createdAt: Date.now(),
        read: false
      };
      const message: BroadcastMessage = { type: 'NEW_NOTIFICATION', notification };
      this.applyMessage(message);        // update THIS tab's own state
      this.channel.postMessage(message); // tell every OTHER tab
    }, 8000);
  }

  markAsRead(id: string): void {
    const message: BroadcastMessage = { type: 'MARK_READ', id };
    this.applyMessage(message);
    this.channel.postMessage(message);
    // A real app would also send a PATCH /notifications/:id/read request here,
    // so the "read" status is remembered even after a refresh.
  }

  // This one function is the single place that actually changes state.
  // It's called the same way whether the change came from this tab directly,
  // or arrived as a broadcast from a different tab.
  private applyMessage(message: BroadcastMessage): void {
    switch (message.type) {
      case 'NEW_NOTIFICATION':
        this.notificationsState.update(list => [message.notification, ...list]);
        if (message.notification.priority === 'urgent') {
          this.toast.show(message.notification.message);
        }
        break;
      case 'MARK_READ':
        this.notificationsState.update(list =>
          list.map(n => (n.id === message.id ? { ...n, read: true } : n))
        );
        break;
      case 'MARK_ALL_READ':
        this.notificationsState.update(list => list.map(n => ({ ...n, read: true })));
        break;
    }
  }
}
```

