## 5. Example Explanation – Execution Flow

Walking through exactly what happens, step by step:

1. **Tab 1 opens.** It fetches its starting data (the seeded array, standing in for a real API call), then asks for the leader lock. Nobody else is asking for it yet, so Tab 1 gets it instantly and becomes the leader. It starts "hearing" a fake notification every 8 seconds.
2. **Tab 2 opens** (same app, second tab). It also fetches starting data, and also asks for the leader lock — but Tab 1 already holds it. So Tab 2's "become leader" code just never runs. Tab 2 quietly becomes a follower and never opens a connection of its own.
3. **A new notification arrives in Tab 1** (the leader, since it's the one "connected"). Tab 1 does two things: updates its own bell count directly, and also posts the notification on the BroadcastChannel for everyone else.
4. **Tab 2 hears that broadcast** through its `channel.onmessage` listener, and runs the exact same update function Tab 1 used. Its bell count goes up by the same amount, at basically the same moment. If the notification was urgent, Tab 2 also pops its own toast.
5. **The user marks something as read, in Tab 2 this time.** Tab 2 updates itself, then broadcasts that fact. Tab 1 hears it and marks the same item read on its side. Both tabs' unread counts drop together.
6. **The user closes Tab 1** (the current leader). The browser automatically frees up the lock. Tab 2, which has been sitting there waiting for that same lock the whole time, immediately gets it, becomes the new leader, and starts its own "connection" — nobody had to write any code to make that handoff happen.

## 6. When should you use this?

- Anything where multiple tabs of the same app need to agree on state that changes often: a notification bell, an unread-messages count, "3 people are viewing this document right now" indicators, a shopping cart total, or things like being logged out in one tab and needing every tab to notice.
- Any live feature (backed by a WebSocket or similar) where it's normal for your users to keep several tabs of your app open — that's when reducing the number of connections actually matters for your server's cost and capacity.
- You probably don't need any of this if the data is cheap to just re-fetch whenever a tab needs it, or if your users basically never have more than one tab of your app open — the extra complexity wouldn't be buying you anything.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**

- Exactly one live connection per person, not per tab — meaningfully cuts down server load for anyone who keeps multiple tabs open.
- Every open tab is always telling the truth, in agreement with every other tab — no "why does one tab say 3 and another says 5" confusion.
- Handing off "who's the leader" when a tab closes is automatic and doesn't need any custom code, because the browser itself enforces who can hold the lock.

**Disadvantages**

- It's a fair bit more moving parts than "just open a socket per tab" — whoever maintains this code needs to actually understand leader election and the message-passing setup, not just skim it.
- If a browser aggressively pauses a background tab to save power, there can be a brief delay before leadership hands off — usually fine in practice, but worth knowing about.
- There's an alternative way to build this using something called a **SharedWorker** (a single background script the browser shares across all tabs of the same site automatically, no election needed), which is arguably a cleaner idea in theory. It's used less often in practice because it's historically had spottier browser support and is harder to debug (you can't always just open devtools and see its console output the way you can a normal tab).

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- **The "broadcast doesn't echo back" trap — the single most common bug here.** `channel.postMessage(...)` only reaches *other* tabs — it does **not** deliver the message back to the very tab that sent it. If your leader's code assumes it'll also hear its own broadcast and update itself that way, the leader's own screen will just never update, even though every other tab is working fine. The fix is what the example code does: apply the change directly, locally, *and* separately broadcast it — two explicit steps, not one.
- **The zone.js / Web Locks trap (Angular-specific).** The function you hand to `navigator.locks.request()` runs in a part of the browser that Angular's automatic change-detection system doesn't know to watch. If you update a signal inside that callback without wrapping it in `NgZone.run(...)`, the value technically changes correctly, but Angular never notices, so the screen just sits there stale. This is a real, easy-to-hit bug, not a theoretical one.
- Treating "just give every tab its own WebSocket" as good enough — it'll technically work, but it's exactly the wasteful, out-of-sync pattern this whole design exists to avoid. Worth saying out loud in an interview even if a simpler version is what you'd actually ship first.
- Forgetting the "fetch current state on startup" step — without it, a tab that opens after other notifications already exist has no way to learn about them, since BroadcastChannel only carries messages sent *while that tab happens to already be open and listening*.
- Reaching for `localStorage`'s built-in `'storage'` event as a shortcut instead of BroadcastChannel — it has the exact same "doesn't fire in the tab that made the change" limitation, plus its own unrelated quirks (size limits, needing to serialize everything to a string). BroadcastChannel is the tool actually built for this job.
- Only updating local/broadcast state when marking something as read, and forgetting to also tell the server (e.g., a `PATCH` request) — broadcasting keeps open tabs in sync with each other, but a page refresh or a brand-new tab needs the server to remember it too.



