applyMessage — the actual "add this to the list, bump the count, maybe show a toast" logic — is written exactly once. It just gets triggered from two different moments:

Right when this tab causes a change itself (the leader hearing from the server, or any tab handling a user click like "mark as read") — called directly, synchronously, no round trip needed.
Whenever some other tab's broadcast arrives — called from onmessage.

So yes, your instinct is correct that the tab causing the change has to update itself separately from broadcasting — postMessage alone will never touch that tab's own UI. But the reason that's not "writing it twice" is that both call sites hand off to the same one function. If you ever need to change what happens when a notification arrives, you change applyMessage once, and both paths — local action and remote broadcast — pick up the fix automatically.

One more precision: this isn't actually leader-specific. It applies to whichever tab originates a change. The leader happens to be the one originating "new notification" events (since it's the one with the connection), but a follower tab is just as capable of originating a "mark as read" event when the user clicks it there — and that follower has to do the exact same "apply locally, then broadcast" two-step, for the exact same reason.

