"I'd use the Web Locks API to elect exactly one tab as the 'leader,' and only that tab opens the real WebSocket connection to the server — every other tab is a follower and opens nothing. The leader shares everything it learns with the other tabs over a BroadcastChannel, and every tab, including the leader, updates its own bell count using the same shared logic, so they can never disagree. If the leader tab closes, the browser frees the lock automatically and another tab takes over with no extra code."

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

"For a live bell icon that stays correct across multiple tabs, I wouldn't just have every tab open its own WebSocket — that means more open connections per user than necessary, and it does nothing to keep read/unread state in sync between tabs anyway. Instead, I'd use the Web Locks API to run a leader election: every tab asks for the same named lock on startup, only one of them gets it, and that one becomes the leader and opens the actual WebSocket or SSE connection. Every other tab is a follower and doesn't connect to anything. The leader shares whatever it hears from the server — new notifications, read-state changes — over a BroadcastChannel, and every tab, leader included, runs the exact same update function in response, so there's no way for tabs to end up disagreeing. Urgent notifications trigger a toast through that same shared function, so every open tab shows the popup, not just whichever one happens to be active. If the leader tab is closed, the browser releases the lock by itself, and whichever follower was waiting immediately becomes the new leader — no manual handoff code needed. One bug worth mentioning: BroadcastChannel never delivers a message back to the tab that sent it, so the leader has to update its own state directly, separately from broadcasting it, or its own screen just won't refresh. And every tab does one normal API call on startup so it isn't starting from a blank slate, and read-state gets saved to the server too, not just shared between tabs, so it survives a refresh."

## 12. Quick Revision

1. **The ask:** live bell updates, urgent toasts, and the unread count staying correct across every open tab.
2. **Why the simple version fails:** one WebSocket per tab wastes server connections, and gives tabs no way to learn what happened in each other — counts drift, alerts get inconsistent.
3. **The fix has two tools:** Web Locks API picks exactly one "leader" tab to hold the real connection; BroadcastChannel lets that leader tell every other tab what it learned.
4. **One shared update function:** every tab (leader or not) reacts to changes the same way, so they can't disagree.
5. **Two traps to remember:** a tab never hears its own broadcast back (update locally *and* broadcast, separately); code inside `navigator.locks.request()` runs outside Angular's zone (wrap signal writes in `NgZone.run()`).
6. **Don't forget:** every tab fetches current state from the server on startup — BroadcastChannel only carries messages sent while that tab was already open.

## 13. Memory Trick

**One antenna, many radios.** Only one tab (the leader) has its antenna plugged into the real broadcast tower (the WebSocket connection to the server). Every tab — including the leader itself — is also just a radio tuned to the same local station (BroadcastChannel), so they all play the exact same thing at the exact same moment. If the antenna-radio gets unplugged (that tab closes), the next radio in line automatically grows its own antenna and takes over — no one has to walk over and plug anything in by hand.
